WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.431
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level A two.

2
00:00:05.731 --> 00:00:08.955
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:15.344
Unit twenty-five: Present Perfect with For / Since. Today you will say how long something has continued.

4
00:00:15.644 --> 00:00:18.227
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.353
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:23.811
For.

7
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:26.723
For means used with a length of time.

8
00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:29.339
I have lived here for five years.

9
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.033
Your turn. Say: For.

10
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:35.564
For.

11
00:00:36.317 --> 00:00:37.318
Since.

12
00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:41.036
Since means used with the starting point of a time.

13
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.230
I have lived here since 2020.

14
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:47.283
Your turn. Say: Since.

15
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:51.053
Since.

16
00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:52.853
Have lived.

17
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.697
Have lived means have been living in a place until now.

18
00:00:57.283 --> 00:00:59.016
I have lived here for years.

19
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:02.002
Your turn. Say: Have lived.

20
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:05.682
Have lived.

21
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:07.278
Has worked.

22
00:01:08.283 --> 00:01:11.014
Has worked means has been doing a job until now.

23
00:01:11.617 --> 00:01:13.268
She has worked here since May.

24
00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:16.501
Your turn. Say: Has worked.

25
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:20.542
Has worked.

26
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:22.551
Period of time.

27
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.769
Period of time means a length of time, such as two weeks.

28
00:01:27.383 --> 00:01:29.186
Two weeks is a period of time.

29
00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:32.703
Your turn. Say: Period of time.

30
00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:36.800
Period of time.

31
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:38.714
Starting point.

32
00:01:39.717 --> 00:01:42.262
Starting point means the moment when something began.

33
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.571
Monday is the starting point.

34
00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:48.300
Your turn. Say: Starting point.

35
00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:52.319
Starting point.

36
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:53.899
Still.

37
00:01:54.883 --> 00:01:56.964
Still means continuing until now.

38
00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:58.969
I still live here.

39
00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:02.085
Your turn. Say: Still.

40
00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:05.667
Still.

41
00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:07.441
So far.

42
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.148
So far means until now.

43
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.214
So far I have finished three units.

44
00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:16.481
Your turn. Say: So far.

45
00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:20.532
So far.

46
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:25.658
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:26.517 --> 00:02:29.213
Visal, how long have you studied English?

48
00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:39.041
I have studied it for two years. We use the present perfect with for and since to talk about something that began in the past and continues now.

49
00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:41.430
What is the difference?

50
00:02:42.517 --> 00:02:57.060
For + a period of time: for two years, for three hours. Since + a starting point: since 2024, since Monday, since I was ten. Both answer the question How long?

51
00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.871
Can we use the past simple?

52
00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:14.363
Only for finished actions: I lived in Phnom Penh for two years, but I don't live there now. With the present perfect, the situation is still true: I have lived here for two years.

53
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.209
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:21.721
Grammar focus: Present perfect with for and since.

55
00:03:22.021 --> 00:03:23.150
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.733
I have lived here for five years.

57
00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.160
She has worked here since 2020.

58
00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.293
They have studied English for two months.

59
00:03:33.233 --> 00:03:40.646
Use for with a period of time and since with a starting point. They are used with the present perfect for situations that continue until now.

60
00:03:41.383 --> 00:03:44.953
Be careful! Don't say: I have lived here since two years.

61
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.782
Say: I have lived here for two years.

62
00:03:50.082 --> 00:03:51.931
Use for with a period of time.

63
00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:55.757
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:56.567 --> 00:03:57.545
How Long?.

65
00:03:57.845 --> 00:04:00.030
I have lived in this town for five years.

66
00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:04.287
My sister has worked at the school since 2020.

67
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.520
We have studied English for two months.

68
00:04:08.533 --> 00:04:10.394
She has been ill since Monday.

69
00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.771
So far we have finished ten units.

70
00:04:14.867 --> 00:04:18.648
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:19.483 --> 00:04:22.110
I have lived here blank 2019.

72
00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.785
A, for. B, since. C, ago.

73
00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:31.333
The answer is B: since.

74
00:04:32.483 --> 00:04:34.425
She has worked here blank three years.

75
00:04:34.725 --> 00:04:38.042
A, for. B, since. C, from.

76
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.193
The answer is A: for.

77
00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.570
They have known each other blank they were children.

78
00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:49.699
A, for. B, since. C, during.

79
00:04:53.233 --> 00:04:55.233
The answer is B: since.

80
00:04:56.383 --> 00:04:58.127
Which sentence is correct?

81
00:04:58.427 --> 00:05:06.736
A, I live here since 2019.. B, I have lived here since 2019.. C, I am living here since 2019..

82
00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:14.169
The answer is B: I have lived here since 2019..

83
00:05:15.317 --> 00:05:19.151
According to the reading, how long has the writer's family lived in the town?

84
00:05:19.451 --> 00:05:24.033
A, five years. B, two months. C, since Monday.

85
00:05:27.567 --> 00:05:29.636
The answer is A: five years.

86
00:05:30.833 --> 00:05:34.229
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:35.083 --> 00:05:38.537
How long have you studied English? Use for or since.

88
00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:48.792
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:49.092 --> 00:06:02.707
I have studied English for three years. I started in 2023, so I have studied it since 2023. I use for with a period of time and since with the starting point.

90
00:06:03.007 --> 00:06:04.647
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:10.281
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present perfect with for and since.

92
00:06:10.581 --> 00:06:14.928
Today's value is perseverance. Keep trying, even when it is hard.

93
00:06:15.228 --> 00:06:18.356
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:06:18.656 --> 00:06:21.669
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

